# THE LIST OF WHAT THIS APPLICATION READS. src/env.ts is where the application
# declares them, each with its check and the default of any that has one; this
# is the same list as a file to copy (src/env.test.ts keeps the two in step).
#
# Nothing that differs from one deployment to the next is assumed. Which
# database, and the secret that signs sessions, are blank here, and the server
# and the commands stop until they are said -- in development too. A default
# that is here is right wherever it is left as it is.
#
# For local development, copy it to .env at the repository's root, beside
# vite.config.ts, and fill in what is blank. `npm run dev` reads it there, and
# so do the commands in scripts/ and the checks in tests/ that reach the
# database (scripts/env.mjs), under whatever the shell sets.
#
# In production nothing reads a .env at all: the service manager supplies these.
# This file is the reference for writing that unit, not the unit itself -- so
# copying it into /etc/sysconfig or the like gets you a starting point, and
# every value still has to be the one this deployment wants. A command run there
# is given the same settings, as with
# `node --env-file=<the server's file> scripts/user.mjs`.
#
# A SECRET CAN BE A FILE INSTEAD. BETTER_AUTH_SECRET_FILE in BETTER_AUTH_SECRET's
# place, and GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY_FILE in GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY's, name a file
# holding the secret, read once at start, the whitespace around it not part of
# it; the database's password is libpq's own file (PGPASSFILE). Set one of a
# secret and its file, never both. An environment is inherited by every process
# started under it, and a systemd unit's Environment= can be read over D-Bus; a
# file is read only by whoever may read it. Under systemd, a credential and its
# path:
#
#   LoadCredential=better-auth-secret:/etc/reckon/better-auth-secret
#   Environment=BETTER_AUTH_SECRET_FILE=%d/better-auth-secret
#
# and under Docker, a secret at /run/secrets/<name>.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

# What signs every session cookie: 32 characters at least, random, and this
# deployment's alone, development's too. The server will not start without one
# -- left unset, Better Auth would sign with a default anybody can read in its
# source, and a dev server the network can reach is no exception.
#
# Changing it signs everybody out, which is also how to do that on purpose.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
#   or, a file holding it:
# BETTER_AUTH_SECRET_FILE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Where it listens
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by adapter-node itself, not by application code.
#
# HOST unset means 0.0.0.0 — reachable from the network. Where a reverse proxy
# is meant to be the only way in, set 127.0.0.1 so the bind enforces that
# rather than a configuration file claiming it.
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Behind the proxy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There is no public address to set. The server reads its own from each
# request -- the Host header, over https -- so it is always the one the browser
# used. The first two are for a proxy that does not pass those through as they
# are; the address header is for any proxy at all. "Serving it" in the README
# has the cases.

# Read by adapter-node: the header carrying the scheme, for a proxy that serves
# plain http. Unset, every request is taken to be https.
# PROTOCOL_HEADER=x-forwarded-proto

# Read by adapter-node: the header carrying the public name, for a proxy that
# does not pass Host through.
# HOST_HEADER=x-forwarded-host

# Read by adapter-node: the header holding the visitor's address. NEEDED BEHIND
# ANY PROXY. Unset, every visitor is the proxy's address: the per-address
# sign-in limit becomes one limit for everyone, and every session records the
# proxy. A header the proxy sets from the connection and overwrites needs
# nothing else; X-Forwarded-For, which a client can start, needs XFF_DEPTH as
# well -- the number of proxies in front.
# ADDRESS_HEADER=x-real-ip
#   or
# ADDRESS_HEADER=x-forwarded-for
# XFF_DEPTH=1

# Whether the session cookie is Secure, so a browser only ever sends it over
# https. false only behind a proxy that serves plain http, where the session
# token then crosses that network in the clear -- and the server says so in
# its log every time it starts.
#
# Dev: if the browser you develop in will not keep a Secure cookie on
# http://localhost, set false in your .env.
SECURE_COOKIES=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------

# Which database: reckon_dev, say, in development, and the deployment's own in
# production. REQUIRED wherever DATABASE_URL is not set: none is assumed, since
# a default here would be one copy away from a process writing invoices into
# the wrong one.
PGDATABASE=

# Where it is. Unset, where libpq looks on this platform: on Linux the unix
# socket /var/run/postgresql, which peer-authenticates -- the role is decided by
# the operating-system user, so there is no password to leak -- /tmp on macOS,
# localhost on Windows. Set it only to point somewhere else.
# PGHOST=

# Who to connect as, its password where the database asks for one, and the
# port. Unset: the operating-system user, no password, 5432. Over the unix
# socket on Linux no password is asked for.
# PGUSER=
# PGPASSWORD=
# PGPORT=5432

# Or the password from libpq's password file, which psql reads too, a line
# host:port:database:user:password for each connection: ~/.pgpass unless set.
# Only its owner may read it, or it is passed over, as libpq passes over it.
# PGPASSFILE=

# Wins outright over PGHOST and PGDATABASE when set. For TCP, another machine,
# or a managed service.
# DATABASE_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — email
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The relay reckon hands its email to: an invitation to someone added on Team,
# a link to choose a new password after a reset, a link to confirm an address,
# and a notice whenever a password changes. Any host that takes mail by SMTP: a
# provider's, or a mail server on this machine or this network. Unset, reckon
# sends no email, nobody can be invited, and a notice waits a week for one to
# be set.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com

# Its port: 587 unless set, 465 where SMTP_SECURITY is tls. A mail server on this
# machine usually takes it on 25.
# SMTP_PORT=587

# How the connection is kept private:
#   starttls  (unset) the relay must upgrade it with STARTTLS, or nothing is sent
#   tls       TLS from the first byte, as port 465 expects
#   none      no TLS: for a relay on this machine, where the mail crosses no
#             network. To any other, the server says so in its log at every start.
# The relay's certificate is checked whichever is chosen.
# SMTP_SECURITY=starttls

# Who to sign in to the relay as, and the password, where it asks -- or the
# password from a file, in its place. A local relay seldom asks.
# SMTP_USERNAME=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/credentials/reckon.service/smtp-password

# For a relay whose certificate a private authority issued: a PEM file of the
# authorities to trust for it, in place of the system's. There is no setting to
# leave the certificate unchecked.
# SMTP_CA_FILE=

# The name it greets the relay with: this machine's own unless set, where that is
# a full one, else its address -- which some relays, Google's among them, take
# as a sign of spam. The domain it sends from will do.
# SMTP_HELO_NAME=

# The address it is sent from, one the relay may send as. Needed with
# SMTP_HOST. It goes under the business's trading name; replies go to the
# business's own email.
# SMTP_FROM=reckon@example.com

# A mail server on this machine, Postfix say, relaying on to a provider:
#   SMTP_HOST=127.0.0.1  SMTP_PORT=25  SMTP_SECURITY=none

# Where reckon is reached. The links it emails begin with this, never with the
# address a request came in on, which a request can say anything about. https,
# except to this machine in development. Unset, nobody can be invited or have a
# password reset by email.
# SITE_URL=https://reckon.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — addresses
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# TWO keys, because a Google API key carries exactly one application
# restriction. A key can be restricted to HTTP referrers or to IP addresses,
# never both -- so a key the browser uses and a key this server uses cannot be
# the same key without one of them being unrestricted.
#
# An address is chosen from Google's suggestions and confirmed by Google before
# it is saved, online only: there is no typed address standing in for a place.
# So both keys are needed for any address to be entered. Without them the
# address field says it cannot look one up, and nothing saves.

# Google Places API (New). Unset, the address field is closed and says why.
#
# THE BROWSER KEY. It reaches the page, because a type-ahead fires on every
# keystroke and proxying would put a round trip to this server in front of each
# one. src/env.ts is what makes it public; the PUBLIC_ prefix is what tells you.
#
#   - Application restriction: HTTP referrers -- this deployment's origin, and
#     any you develop from, such as http://localhost:5173/*
#   - API restriction: Maps JavaScript API, Places API (New)
#
# Both APIs, and the JavaScript one is not optional -- it is what makes the
# referrer restriction work at all. Google applies referrer restrictions to the
# JavaScript API because browsers send the header, while a direct web-service
# call wants an IP restriction, which cannot mean anything when the caller is
# somebody else's browser.
PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=

# THE SERVER KEY. Private in src/env.ts, so it never reaches a page.
#
#   - Application restriction: IP addresses -- this host's IPv4 address and its
#     IPv6 /64, since a host with IPv6 reaches Google over it from addresses
#     that change within the prefix
#   - API restriction: Address Validation API, Places API (New), Routes API
#
# Three APIs, for jobs it does once per address or per trip, never per
# keystroke:
#
#   Address Validation -- picking a real place says the place exists; this says
#   the address is deliverable and hands back a corrected form, which is worth
#   having on something a tax district is resolved from.
#
#   Places Details -- the browser supplies the place id, and the server does
#   not take its word for it. This asks Google whether the id is a place, and
#   only a place Google confirms is saved.
#
#   Routes -- the miles of each drive of a trip, by Google's route.
#
# If Places API (New) is left off this key, Google refuses every place check
# (API_KEY_SERVICE_BLOCKED) and no address saves; why is in the server's log.
GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
#   or, a file holding it:
# GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY_FILE=
